====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|in many religions|often|capitalized}} A place of [[torment]] where some or all [[sinner]]s are believed to go after [[death]] and [[evil]] [[spirit]]s are believed to be.
#: {{ant|en|heaven}}
#: {{ux|en|May you rot in '''hell'''!}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Proverbs|23|14|text=Thou shalt beat him with the rod, and shalt deliver his soul from '''hell'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1667|author=John Milton|title=Paradise Lost|passage=Better to reign in '''Hell''' than serve in '''Heaven'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Congreve Mourning Bride|page=39|passage=Heav'n has no Rage, like Love to Hatred turn'd, / Nor '''Hell''' a Fury, like a Woman ſcorn'd.}}
#* {{RQ:Joyce Portrait|passage='''Hell''' is a strait and dark and foul-smelling prison, an abode of demons and lost souls, filled with fire and smoke.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2012|album=w:Collide with the Sky|artist=w:Pierce the Veil|title='''Hell''' Above|passage='Cause this is a wasteland, my only retreat / With heaven above you, there's '''hell''' over me}}
# {{lb|en|in many religions|often|capitalized|inexact}} {{synonym of|en|underworld}}{{ngd|, an [[underground]] [[land]] of the [[dead]] without [[eternal]] [[punishment]] or with [[only]] [[some]] [[region]]s of [[punishment]].}}

=====Usage notes=====
In English, ''Hell'' or ''hell'' is typically conflated with present Christian and Islamic beliefs about a fiery [[underworld]] of eternal punishment inflicted by [[demon]]s on the wicked, which may or may not include [[Limbo]], a realm for the virtuous dead of other faiths. Similar beliefs in other traditions are often{{mdash}}but not univerally{{mdash}}distinguished by using specific alternative names (e.g. ''{{w|Diyu}}'' for the traditional Chinese underworld of punishments for the wicked) or by distinguishing the specific form of ''hell'' intended, as with calling [[Naraka]] the ''[[Buddhist]] Hell'' or ''Hells''. Often within these traditions and occasionally within Christian and Islamic sects, the land of punishment is only a temporary realm of purification of the spirit, similar to [[Purgatory]] in [[Catholicism]].

Informally, any underground [[afterworld]] may be called ''Hell'' but scholarly treatment usually distinguishes such neutral and general beliefs as the Hebrew [[Sheol]], the Greco-Roman [[Hades]], and Sumerian [[Kur]] with more neutral terms, such as {{m|en|underworld}}, {{m|en|afterlife}}, or ''[[land]] of the [[dead]]''.

